<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Constantinople (1700)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1700">1700</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1686-1700">Russo-Turkish War (1686–1700)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Constantinople of July 1700 ended the war of 1686–1700 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Russia kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a>, taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> in 1696, and Muscovy was released from paying the annual 'gifts' to the Crimean khans. The thirty-year truce freed Peter to begin the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>As a member of the anti-Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a>, the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a>. When the other members, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, had reached their war aims, they made peace with Sultan Mustafa II at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlowitz</a> in 1699, a settlement that ignored Russian interests.</p><p>Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> joined the negotiations in person and agreed a two-year truce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlowitz</a>. A final settlement was prevented by the gap between his demands, which included protection of the Christians in the Ottoman Balkan provinces, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>’s military position, and by the lack of support from the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>In the autumn of 1699 Peter sent Yemelyan Ignatievich Ukraintsev to the sultan’s court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The Russian aims were Ottoman recognition of the gains around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> and free passage for Russian merchant ships into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The talks moved slowly, and as Peter came under pressure to attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> he urged Ukraintsev to settle, and the demand for Black Sea access was dropped.</p><p>Concluded on 13 July 1700, the treaty set a truce of thirty years and recognised Russian possession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> area, including the new fortresses of Taganrog, Pavlovsk and Mius. Russia gave up its claims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Kerch Strait</a> but was relieved of the annual payment to the Crimean Khanate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> fortresses razed by Russian forces went back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and the lower Dnieper area became a demilitarised zone. Russian pilgrims gained free passage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, and Russia gained a diplomatic representative in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Peter had arranged a three-front attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> with August the Strong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and Frederik IV of Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, but his own part was to begin only once peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> was made. The delay meant that Denmark-Norway was already defeated when his army left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and Sweden was able to repulse the Russian attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> nine years later, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a>, beaten by Peter at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Battle of Poltava</a>, camped the remnants of his army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Bender</a>. The sultan declared war, and the treaty was superseded by the Treaty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Pruth</a>, which returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> to the sultan, and then by the Treaty of Adrianople of 1713, which restored a peace intended to last twenty-five years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Constantinople (1700)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muscovy kept paying the Crimean khans regular ‘gifts’, in effect tribute, until the <b>Treaty of Constantinople</b> in 1700, long after the Golden Horde had vanished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
